{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's defense relies on a false dichotomy between 'rational belief' and 'rational action,' failing to address that the specific act of 'preferring' one theory for action logically entails an expectation of superior future performance. If the agent acts on Theory A over Theory B because A is better corroborated, the agent is effectively betting that A will not fail immediately; this bet constitutes an inductive inference about the future based on the past, regardless of whether the agent labels it 'probability.' By defining corroboration strictly as a 'report of past performance' while simultaneously justifying it as the basis for 'selecting the most informative target,' the target smuggles induction back in under the guise of methodology: one cannot rationally select a tool for future use based solely on past survival without implicitly assuming that past survival is a relevant indicator of future utility, which is the very principle of uniformity Popper sought to reject.","premise":"Preferring a theory for future action based on past survival implicitly assumes that past survival predicts future reliability.","premises_essential":["84341236b55ff7a360a49cea17f189ea247a4ce5d7b35f6ae10c00f23a344227"],"successor_question":"Can a methodological rule for selecting theories be formulated that utilizes past test results without implying any expectation that the selected theory will perform better than unselected ones in the immediate future?","target":"84341236b55ff7a360a49cea17f189ea247a4ce5d7b35f6ae10c00f23a344227"}]}